=== task: multi medium
title: A separate copy
prompt: `a` holds `[1, 2, 3]`. Choose **every** line that makes `b` a **separate** list with the same items, in the same order.
- `b = a` :: This makes b a second name for the same list. A change through one name shows up through the other.
* `b = a.copy()` :: Right. copy() builds a new list with the same items.
* `b = a[:]` :: Right. A full slice is a new list with all the items.
- `b = a.sort()` :: sort() changes a in place and gives back None, so b would be None.
- `b = a[::-1]` :: That's a new list, but the items are in reverse order.
* `b = a[0:]` :: Right. From index 0 to the end is a full slice: a new list with all the items.
hint: A slice of a list is a new list. Plain `=` never copies.
hint: Three lines are correct.
right: `a.copy()`, `a[:]` and `a[0:]` each build a new list with the same items in the same order. `b = a` only adds a second name, `sort()` gives back None, and `[::-1]` reverses the order.
wrong: Only a copy gives a separate list: `a.copy()`, or a slice of the whole list (`a[:]`, `a[0:]`). `b = a` shares the list, `a.sort()` returns None, and `a[::-1]` is reversed.

