```
labels: nums[0] | nums[1] | nums[2] | nums[3] | nums[4]
answer: 4 | 2 | 1 | 3 | 5
wrong[1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5]: The even numbers are inserted at the front, not appended at the end. 2 goes in front of 1, and later 4 goes in front of everything.
wrong[2 | 4 | 1 | 3 | 5]: Each insert(0, ...) puts the new number in front of everything, including the 2 that's already at the front. So 4 ends up before 2.
wrong[4 | 2 | 5 | 3 | 1]: The odd numbers are appended at the end, so they stay in the order they arrive: 1, then 3, then 5.
