hint: Track the list after each iteration: [1], then [2, 1], then ...
right: [1] → [2, 1] → [2, 1, 3] → [4, 2, 1, 3] → [4, 2, 1, 3, 5]. Each insert at index 0 pushes everything else one place right.
wrong: Go one iteration at a time: 1 is appended ([1]), 2 goes in front ([2, 1]), 3 is appended ([2, 1, 3]), 4 goes in front ([4, 2, 1, 3]), and 5 is appended ([4, 2, 1, 3, 5]).

=== task: code hard
title: Fix the list bugs
prompt: This program should add two scores, sort the list, and print the best score. It should print:
```output
[60, 70, 75, 85, 90]
Best: 90
```
It has three bugs. Fix them.
starter:
```python
scores = [70, 85, 60]
scores.append(90, 75)
scores = scores.sort()
print(scores)
print("Best:", scores[len(scores)])
```
solution:
```python
scores = [70, 85, 60]
scores.append(90)
scores.append(75)
scores.sort()
print(scores)
print("Best:", scores[-1])
```
tests: stdout
error[TypeError]: `append` takes exactly one item, so add the two scores with two separate appends. If the message mentions NoneType, `scores = scores.sort()` stored None in `scores`: `sort()` changes the list itself and gives back None, so write `scores.sort()` on its own.
error[IndexError]: `scores[len(scores)]` is one past the end. The last item, the best score after sorting, is `scores[-1]`.
hint: Python stops at line 2 first: how many items can `append` add at once?
hint: sort() gives back None. What ends up in `scores` on line 3?
hint: After sorting, the best score is the last item.
right: Two appends add the two scores, `scores.sort()` on its own sorts the list in place (assigning its result would store None), and `scores[-1]` is the last item of the sorted list, the best score.
wrong: Bug 1: `append` adds one item, so call it twice. Bug 2: write `scores.sort()` without `scores =`, because sort() returns None. Bug 3: `scores[len(scores)]` is past the end; use `scores[-1]`.

